by the Author on the Mod's official project pages.


1. WHAT YOU MAY DO

  a) Read, study, build and run the source code.

  b) Fork the repository and publish your fork's source code, for example
     on GitHub, as long as this license file stays with it unchanged.

  c) Modify the Mod and build it for your own use, including on servers
     you run and in modpacks that you do not publish.

  d) Give a build of a modified Mod to the players of a server you run, so
     they can play on it, or to a small group of people for testing. You
     may send it directly or through the server's own channels, such as its
     website, Discord or launcher, or in a modpack made for the server, as
     long as neither the build nor the pack is listed on a mod hosting site
     or offered to the public as a download.

  e) Submit changes to the Mod, such as pull requests, issues with patches
     or other code (see section 3).

  f) Include unmodified Official Releases in a modpack, including a public
     one, or point to them from it.

  g) Use small portions of the code, such as single methods or techniques,
     in your own distinct project, with credit to the Mod and its
     contributors and a link to this repository, as long as that project
     is not a substitute for the Mod or for one of its features as a whole.

  h) Let an automated build service, such as GitHub Actions, build your
     fork or your pull request and keep the builds as temporary build
     artifacts of that service, for testing and review. This does not
     allow attaching them to releases or linking to them as downloads.


2. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

  a) Publish a compiled build (such as a .jar file) of a modified version
     of the Mod, or of a fork of it, anywhere the public can find or
     download it. This covers mod hosting sites (such as CurseForge,
     Modrinth and Planet Minecraft), launchers, mod portals, download sites
     and release pages, including GitHub Releases. It also covers public
     modpacks that contain such a build. The only exceptions are the cases
     allowed by 1d and 1h.

  b) Re-upload Official Releases on their own, outside a modpack, to any
     site or service.

  c) Publish the Mod, a fork of it, or a substantial part of it, under a
     different name or as your own project, whether as a build or as a
     project page on a mod hosting site.

  d) Sell the Mod or a fork of it, or put access to it behind a payment.

  e) Remove or change copyright notices, credits or this license.

If you want to do any of the above, ask the Author. Permission is only
given in writing, by the Author, and only for what it names.


